WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
Starlink V3 satellites imaging starship V3 in orbit, tightens the feedback loop between constellation hardware and next block vehicle development. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 53. A next generation starlink satellite captured imagery of starship V3 while both vehicles were in orbit. The sighting confirmed starship V3 reached orbital altitude during its test campaign. Reports indicate the starlink V3 unit used upgraded imaging

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:53.520
sensors to document the upper stage. Observers note the event occurred on the same day as ongoing block 3 flight preparations at starbase. Next steps include verification of vehicle attitude and thermal performance from the new vantage point. While orbital imagery tightens the starship development loop, ground infrastructure at starbase is also being upgraded to match.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:01:15.820
Crews continue structural and plumbing upgrades at starbase pad 1, while pad 2 supports the first 2 block 3 flights. Retrofit work focuses on flame trench modifications and quick disconnect hardware rated for higher propellant flow rates. The changes align pad 1 with the increased thrust and mass of starship V3 vehicles.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:40.120
Completion timelines remain tied to the next static fire window. Pad 1 work proceeds in parallel with active operations at the newer pad. With launch pads being readied, aerodynamic data from wind tunnel testing will help define how the upgraded vehicles fly. NASA and SpaceX completed additional wind tunnel runs on starship configurations at multiple MAC numbers.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:58.300
The test measured aerodynamic forces on both the super heavy booster and ship upper stage with updated tile layouts. Data will refine reentry trajectory models for the next flight test window. Engineers compared results against prior sub-scale models to validate scaling assumptions.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:15.520
The joint effort supplies updated coefficients for guidance and thermal protection analysis. From an engineering standpoint, wind tunnel testing of starship configurations measures lift, drag, and moment coefficients across the MAC range the vehicle will encounter during reentry.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:39.440
The test isolate the effect of tile gap geometry and surface roughness on boundary layer transition, which directly controls local heating rates. By comparing full scale derived models against earlier sub-scale runs, engineers quantify how Reynolds number scaling alters the location of transition and therefore the total integrated heat load on the heat shield tiles.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:46.720
Updated tile layouts tested in the latest campaign reflect changes made after flight 13 data review.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:57.700
Reducing uncertainty in those coefficients shrinks the propellant margin required for the deorbit burn and the subsequent bank angle profile that keeps peak heating within tile limits.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:09.740
The same data set feeds the guidance algorithm that commands the ship's reaction control thrusters and body flaps tightening the cord or the vehicle must fly to survive peak dynamic pressure.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:23.120
When the measured aerodynamic database narrows the predicted heating envelope, the design can either reduce tile thickness, lowering mass, or accept a higher entry velocity that would otherwise exceed current thermal margins.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:36.180
Either outcome improves payload to orbit or increases the number of reuses before refurbishment directly attacking the cost per kilogram floor that determines whether rapid reuse is economically viable.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:46.960
The wind tunnel results also inform the next round of computational fluid dynamics validation runs that will be used to certify the block three heat shield configuration.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:58.640
Historical parallels exist in how early reusable launch vehicles refine their thermal protection databases through iterative ground testing before achieving operational reuse rates.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:14.640
Starships approach follows the same logic, close the gap between predicted and actual heating so that the idiot index the ratio of finished heat shield cost to raw material and energy inputs can be driven down through manufacturing scale and reduced over design.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:23.300
Continued tunnel work therefore serves as the immediate lever for both performance and economics on the path to routine orbital operations.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:32.620
High speed footage released from recent starship tests shows the ship upper stage separating while the super heavy booster engines remain lit.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:39.100
The sequence captures the precise timing of hot staging ignition and the resulting plume interaction.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:45.720
Engineers use the video to verify separation dynamics and engine gimbal response under load.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:52.580
The released clip provides direct visual confirmation of the staging sequence used on flight 13.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:58.860
The same staging sequence the wind tunnel models assume was recently captured on high speed video.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:05.340
A Falcon 9 lifted off from Vandenberg Space Force Base carrying 24 Starlink satellites.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.820
The mission used the first stage booster on a return to launch site profile after payload deployment.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:20.000
Launch occurred on the evening of July 31 with real-time webcast coverage confirming stage separation and fairing recovery.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:25.200
The satellites will join the existing constellation to expand coverage density.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:30.220
Ground teams reported nominal performance across all phases of the flight.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:38.740
A second Falcon 9 mission from Vandenberg was scheduled for the evening of August 1 with another batch of 24 Starlink satellites.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:44.380
The launch uses the same booster turnaround process demonstrated on the prior flight.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.360
Ground teams reported no holds during pre-launch checks.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:52.820
The mission continues the rapid reuse cadence at the California site.

00:05:52.820 --> 00:06:00.200
SpaceX confirmed an upcoming Falcon 9 mission from Vandenberg after the July 31 Starlink flight.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:06.820
The manifest continues the site's role in polar orbit deployments separate from Starbase Starship operations.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.880
The additional mission underscores the dual site launch strategy now in routine use.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:20.060
While Starship hardware and testing move forward, Falcon operations continue their steady cadence on the other coast.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:27.560
SpaceX plans to fly initial hardware for orbital data center demonstrations no earlier than 2027.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:36.460
The components would leverage Starlink laser links for intersatellite data routing and power from solar arrays sized for continuous compute loads.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:41.200
Development remains dependent on Starship orbital refueling milestones.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:46.420
The timeline aligns with broader efforts to place compute nodes in low-earth orbit.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:54.460
The same Starlink constellation now supporting vehicle imagery is also being positioned as the backbone for future orbital compute.

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:59.800
On the AI front, SpaceX AI added another data center building in Memphis,

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:05.380
drawing local reaction over construction scale and power demand from the growing cluster.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:13.300
SpaceX delayed removal of XAI's unpermitted temporary turbines in Memphis until 2027,

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:18.000
while permanent power infrastructure is completed for the Colossus expansion.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:27.380
Investors submitted questions on Starship, Starlink, and AI infrastructure through the GROC Q&amp;A portal ahead of SPCX earnings.

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:39.540
Worth keeping an eye on, a contractor filed a $137 million lean against the SpaceX AI site in Memphis, citing unpaid work on data center construction.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:46.780
The claim adds to earlier reports of more than $130 million in disputed invoices from multiple firms.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:54.900
Resolution depends on payment negotiations or formal dispute proceedings that could affect construction pace for the expanding facility.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:58.480
Local officials have not yet commented on the filing.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.560
On the ground, the terrestrial side of that compute push is drawing fresh scrutiny.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:11.640
Reddit users discussed the engineering trade-offs of towing a spent Starship upper stage across open ocean after splashdown,

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:17.100
focusing on toe-point loads and sea-state limits that affect recovery feasibility.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:27.820
Community members shared unverified imagery from a Starlink V3 satellite showing Starship in orbit and debated pixel-scale resolution limits of the onboard cameras.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:34.400
Observers noted the visual differences between the latest hot staging plume and earlier test footage,

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:39.260
attributing changes to updated engine throttling profiles during separation.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:47.200
Before we go, the next static fire window will show whether pad 1 retrofits keep pace with block 3 flight preparations,

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.520
and that's a wrap on today's SpaceX developments.

